.TH SHM_OPEN 2
.SH NAME
shm_open, shm_unlink \- create, open, and remove POSIX shared memory objects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/mman.h>
.B #include <fcntl.h>
.PP
.B int shm_open(const char *name, int oflag, mode_t mode);
.B int shm_unlink(const char *name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Shm_open
opens a kernel-resident shared memory object and returns a file descriptor.
The object name begins with one slash, contains no other slash, and is at most
31 characters long including the leading slash.
No filesystem directory such as
.B /dev/shm
is required.
.PP
.I Oflag
must select
.B O_RDONLY
or
.BR O_RDWR .
It may also contain
.BR O_CREAT ,
.BR O_EXCL ,
and
.BR O_TRUNC .
New objects have length zero and permissions
.I mode
modified by the process umask.
.B O_TRUNC
requires
.BR O_RDWR .
.PP
Use
.IR ftruncate (2)
to set the object length and
.IR mmap (2)
to create
.B MAP_SHARED
or
.B MAP_PRIVATE
mappings.
Objects are limited to 64 MiB.
Shared mappings refer to one VM object across processes; private mappings use
copy-on-write.
Shrinking an object invalidates complete pages past the new end, while growing
it exposes zero-filled bytes.
.PP
.I Shm_unlink
removes the name immediately.
Open descriptors and existing mappings remain valid until their last
reference is released, and the same name may be used for a new independent
object meanwhile.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.I Shm_open
returns a non-negative descriptor on success and -1 on error.
.I Shm_unlink
returns zero on success and -1 on error.
.SH ERRORS
Errors include
.B EACCES
for denied access,
.B EEXIST
for an exclusive create of an existing name,
.B EINVAL
for invalid flags or names,
.B EFBIG
when a requested object length exceeds 64 MiB,
.B ENAMETOOLONG
for an overlong name,
.B ENOENT
when an object is absent,
.B ENOSPC
when the shared-memory object table is full, and the usual descriptor and VM
resource errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
fstat(2), ftruncate(2), mmap(2), umask(2)
